        "item": {
          "clientId": null,
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Define $f_{n}:[0,1] \\\\rightarrow \\\\mathbb{R}$ by the equation $f_{n}(x)=x^{n}$. Show that the sequence $\\\\left(f_{n}\\\\right)$ does not converge uniformly.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"topology\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"For each \\\\(x\\\\in[0,1)\\\\), we have \\\\(x^n\\\\to0\\\\), while \\\\(f_n(1)=1\\\\) for every \\\\(n\\\\). Thus the pointwise limit is\\n\\\\[\\nf(x)=\\\\begin{cases}0,&0\\\\le x<1,\\\\\\\\1,&x=1.\\\\end{cases}\\n\\\\]\\nAny uniform limit would necessarily equal this pointwise limit. Fix \\\\(\\\\varepsilon=\\\\tfrac12\\\\). For every \\\\(N\\\\in\\\\mathbb Z_+\\\\), let \\\\(n=N\\\\) and \\\\(x=2^{-1/N}\\\\). Then \\\\(x<1\\\\), so \\\\(f(x)=0\\\\), whereas\\n\\\\[\\n|f_n(x)-f(x)|=x^N=\\\\frac12.\\n\\\\]\\nHence for every \\\\(N\\\\) there exist \\\\(n\\\\ge N\\\\) and \\\\(x\\\\in[0,1]\\\\) such that \\\\(|f_n(x)-f(x)|\\\\ge\\\\tfrac12\\\\). Therefore \\\\((f_n)\\\\) does not converge uniformly on \\\\([0,1]\\\\).\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
          "id": "01a09487-6219-7d21-a2ec-8a3e8bc0952e",
          "type": "userMessage"
        },
